{"profiles":[{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Arnhold's Mouse Lemur","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Montagne d'Ambre Mouse Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Montagne d’Ambre Mouse Lemur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Arnhold’s Mouse Lemur","source":"Cheirogaleidae"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Microcebus arnholdi","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Microcebus arnholdi Louis Jr., Engberg, McGuire, McCormick, Randriamampionona, Ranaivoarisoa, Bailey et al., 2008","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":8219027},"ottId":5149,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Microcebus arnholdi","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Arnhold's mouse lemur or the Montagne d'Ambre mouse lemur (Microcebus arnholdi) is a species of mouse lemur endemic to Madagascar. Its holotype was first collected on 27 November 2005, and was first described in 2008.","revisionId":1356977617,"title":"Arnhold's mouse lemur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnhold%27s_mouse_lemur","wikidataId":"Q1134080"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Ayres Black Uakari","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Aracá Uakari","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Araçá Uacari","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Ayres' Black Uacari","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Cacajao ayresi","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Cacajao ayresi Boubli, Silva, Amado, Hrbek, Pontual & Farias, 2008","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":5786471},"ottId":6557,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Cacajao ayresi","synonyms":["Cacajao melanocephalus ayresi"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The Aracá uakari (Cacajao ayresi), also known as the Ayres black uakari, is a newly described species of monkey from the northwest Brazilian Amazon. It was found by Jean-Phillipe Boubli of the University of Auckland after following native Yanomamo Indians on their hunts along the Rio Aracá, a northern tributary of the Rio Negro.","revisionId":1356933470,"title":"Aracá uakari","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arac%C3%A1_uakari","wikidataId":"Q141453"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"François’s Langur","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Fran","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Tonkin Langur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"François Langur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Francois' Langur","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"François' Langur","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Trachypithecus francoisi","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Trachypithecus francoisi (de Pousargues, 1898)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2436539},"ottId":43805,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Trachypithecus francoisi","synonyms":["Semnopithecus francoisi","Trachypithecus francoisi francoisi"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"François' langur (Trachypithecus francoisi), also known as Francois' leaf monkey, the Tonkin leaf monkey, or the white side-burned black langur is a species of Old World monkey and the type species of its species group. It is one of the least studied of the species belonging to the Colobinae subfamily.","revisionId":1356935940,"title":"François' langur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7ois%27_langur","wikidataId":"Q545897"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Albatrosses","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"albatross","source":"The Paleobiology Database"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Diomedeidae","confidence":94,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Diomedeidae","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":5238},"ottId":85277,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Diomedeidae","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Albatrosses, of the biological family Diomedeidae, are large seabirds related to the procellariids, storm petrels, and diving petrels in the order Procellariiformes (the tubenoses). They range widely in the Southern Ocean and the North Pacific.","revisionId":1367213464,"title":"Albatross","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albatross","wikidataId":"Q55805"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Opisthokont","source":"Wikipedia"}],"ottId":332573,"scientificName":"Opisthokonta","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The opisthokonts (from Ancient Greek ὀπίσθιος (opísthios) 'rear, posterior' and κοντός (kontós) 'pole, flagellum') are a broad group of eukaryotes, including both the animal and fungus kingdoms. The opisthokonts, previously called the \"Fungi/Metazoa group\", are generally recognized as a clade.","revisionId":1363243090,"title":"Opisthokont","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opisthokont","wikidataId":"Q129021"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Ibises And Spoonbills","source":"Checklist of Vermont Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Ibises","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Spoonbills","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"ibises & spoonbills","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Ibis","source":"Martha's Vineyard species checklist"},{"language":"en","name":"Ibises, Spoonbills","source":"IOC World Bird List, v"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Threskiornithidae","confidence":94,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Threskiornithidae","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":5288},"ottId":480157,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Threskiornithidae","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The family Threskiornithidae includes 35 extant species of large wading birds, and one more that became extinct in historical times. The family has been traditionally classified into two subfamilies, the ibises and the spoonbills; however, recent genetic studies have cast doubt on this arrangement, and have found the spoonbills to be nested within the Old World ibises, and the New World ibises as an early offshoot.","revisionId":1360742220,"t