 only in Africa, south of the Sahara Desert, and are the only cranes that can nest in trees.","revisionId":1355519166,"title":"Balearica","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balearica","wikidataId":"Q789358"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Asity","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"asities","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Philepittidae","confidence":94,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Philepittidae","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":9332},"ottId":991709,"rank":"family","scientificName":"Philepittidae","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The asities are a family of birds, Philepittidae, that are endemic to Madagascar. The asities consist of four species in two genera.","revisionId":1370133727,"title":"Asity","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asity","wikidataId":"Q756777"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Owls","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Goatsuckers","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Owl","source":"Martha's Vineyard species checklist"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Strigiformes","confidence":94,"rank":"order","scientificName":"Strigiformes","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":1450},"ottId":1028829,"rank":"order","scientificName":"Strigiformes","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Owls are birds from the order Strigiformes (), which includes over 200 species of mostly solitary and nocturnal birds of prey typified by an upright stance, a large, broad head, binocular vision, binaural hearing, sharp talons, and feathers adapted for silent flight. Exceptions include the diurnal northern hawk-owl and the gregarious burrowing owl.","revisionId":1376398163,"title":"Owl","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owl","wikidataId":"Q25222"}},{"commonNames":[],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Archaeopsittacus","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Archaeopsittacus Lambrecht, 1933","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":4843181},"ottId":3603357,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Archae